WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 61: The 1911 Inaugural Indianapolis 500: Ray Harroun and the Marmon Wasp

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.120
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:18.320
[Rebecca] On Memorial Day, May 30, 1911, 80,000 roaring spectators gathered at the newly paved Indianapolis Motor Speedway to witness the birth of a motorsport legend.

4
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:26.480
[Rebecca] Forty Thunderous, open cockpit race cars took the green flag for the inaugural international 500 mile sweepstakes.

5
00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:33.360
[Rebecca] While every other car carried two men, a driver and a riding mechanic, whose job was to watch for overtaking cars.

6
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:41.760
[Rebecca] A calm, 32-year-old automotive engineer named Ray Harron showed up alone in a sleek, yellow single-seat racer called the Marmon Wasp.

7
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:51.920
[Rebecca] To see who was coming up behind him, Haron mounted a small piece of glass on his dashboard, inventing the automobile rearview mirror and driving straight into sporting immortality.

8
00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:56.640
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

9
00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:19.280
[George] America had virtually no paved roads suitable for testing high-speed automotive engineering.

10
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:26.640
[George] In 1909, an energetic, eccentric Indiana entrepreneur and bicycle racing champion named Carl G.

11
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.600
[George] Fisher gathered three fellow Indianapolis businessmen to solve the problem.

12
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:45.200
[George] Fisher purchased 328 acres of flat farmland five miles northwest of downtown Indianapolis and constructed a colossal two and a half mile rectangular oval racetrack with sweeping banked corners.

13
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:55.280
[George] Originally surfaced with crushed stone and 220,000 gallons of heavy road oil, the track hosted its first races in August 1909.

14
00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.000
[George] The result was an unmitigated disaster.

15
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:10.200
[George] The flying stone surface disintegrated under the spinning wheels of heavy race cars, blinding drivers in dense dust clouds, causing catastrophic crashes and claiming the lives of five people.

16
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:16.680
[George] Realizing the track must be completely rebuilt or abandoned, Fisher made a staggering financial gamble.

17
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:26.200
[George] He repaved the entire two and a half-mile oval with 3,200,000 tenpound culinary vitrified paving bricks, bonded with liquid cement.

18
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.840
[George] The legendary brickyard was born.

19
00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:40.040
[George] The roar of racing engines echoed across the Indiana cornfields, signaling the rise of Indianapolis as the undisputed automotive manufacturing capital of the early 20th century.

20
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:54.280
[George] Carl Fischer was a charismatic promoter with an iron will, having made millions through the manufacture of Presto Light Acetylene automobile headlights before dedicating his fortune to testing and advancing automotive engineering on the open road.

21
00:02:54.280 --> 00:03:06.360
[George] By late 1910, Fisher and his management team realized that staging weekend series of short sprint races was failing to generate national enthusiasm or substantial ticket revenues.

22
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:23.800
[George] Fisher proposed a revolutionary concept, scrap the minor weekend events and stage a single, colossal annual spectacle, a grueling 500-mile endurance race with an unprecedented prize purse of $25,000 in gold, equivalent to a fortune today.

23
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:37.320
[George] The 500-mile distance was calculated to push human endurance and mechanical metallurgy to their absolute limits, requiring cars to race at sustained speeds over 70 miles an hour for nearly seven continuous hours.

24
00:03:37.320 --> 00:03:40.280
[George] The event captured international attention.

25
00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:55.520
[George] European racing dynasties entered champion cars, fiery Italian Fiats, roaring German Mercedes, and sleek French Peugeot, alongside American engineering marvels from Case, National, Lozier, Simplex, and Marmon.

26
00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:02.480
[George] On the morning of May 30, 1911, special passenger trains from Chicago, New York, and St.

27
00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:10.400
[George] Louis disgorged over 80,000 spectators, packing the wooden grandstands in the largest sporting gathering in American history.

28
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:22.560
[George] Specially chartered Pullman trains lined the sidings, disgorging wealthy industrialists, European dignitaries, and working class racing fans eager to witness the ultimate test of man and machine.

29
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:34.480
[George] Early settlers faced immense hardships carving farms and homesteads from the dense eastern woodlands, battling disease, inclement weather, and frontier isolation with unyielding resolve.

30
00:04:34.480 --> 00:04:50.480
[George] European road racers and American dirt track drivers alike warned that running 500 continuous miles on a surface composed of sharp paving bricks would destroy tires and shatter suspensions, predicting that fewer than 10 cars would finish the punishing contest.

31
00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:58.880
[George] Among the 40 qualified entries was the Marman Motor Car Company, an elite, high precision engineering firm based in Indianapolis.

32
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:03.600
[George] Marmon's chief engineer and test driver was 32 year old Ray Harron.

33
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:13.440
[George] Born in Pennsylvania, Harron was a mild mannered, intellectual bachelor with a degree in engineering who had won the national driving championship in 1910.

34
00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:23.120
[George] Haron had formally announced his retirement from racing to focus on designing production road cars, but Howard Marman coaxed him back for one final competition.

35
00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:38.520
[George] Harron designed the race car himself, the Marmon Model 32, nicknamed the Wasp because of its bright yellow paint, sharp black pinstripes, and an aerodynamic tapered tail that terminated in a long, horizontal stabilization fin.

36
00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:43.720
[George] But Haron's car caused an immediate, explosive controversy in the garage area.

37
00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:49.240
[George] Every other car on the starting grid was a two-seater carrying a driver and a riding mechanic.

38
00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:53.000
[George] In 1911, riding mechanics were indispensable.

39
00:05:53.000 --> 00:06:06.360
[George] They manually pumped oil pressure with a hand pump, monitored fuel gauges, tightened loose engine bolts with wrenches at high speed, and constantly peered over their shoulders to warn the driver of cars attempting to pass.

40
00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:12.040
[George] Haron's Marmon Wasp was a narrow, lightweight single-seater with room for only the driver.

41
00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:23.960
[George] The Marman Wasp was built with aircraft grade aluminum and hand forged steel alloy, featuring a six-cylinder 477 cubic inch engine that purred with Swisswatch precision.

42
00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:34.920
[George] The spirit of American innovation was evident in every workshop and meeting hall, where practical mechanics and visionary thinkers sought solutions to the most challenging obstacles of their era.

43
00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:49.960
[George] Marmon chief engineer Howard Marman chose a six-cylinder T-head engine that produced a modest 85 horsepower, prioritizing mechanical reliability, balanced weight distribution, and reduced tire wear over raw brute top speed.

44
00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:57.160
[George] Rival drivers erupted in fury, petitioning speedway officials to disqualify the Marman Wasp on safety grounds.

45
00:06:57.160 --> 00:07:05.960
[George] They argued that a lone driver without a riding mechanic would be unable to see overtaking vehicles and would cause fatal pile ups on the crowded brick oval.

46
00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:08.080
[George] Haron remained unperturbed.

47
00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:18.720
[George] Remembering a horse-drawn carriage driver he had seen in Chicago in 1904 who had a small mirror mounted to his dashboard to watch traffic, Haron went to a local hardware shop.

48
00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:33.600
[George] He purchased a 3x8-inch rectangular glass mirror, mounted it inside a steel bracket, and fastened it to four metal struts perched directly above the center of his dashboard, angled so he could see the track behind him without turning his head.

49
00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:42.880
[George] Speedway judges inspected the contraption, recognized that Haron had an unobstructed view of approaching traffic, and allowed the Marmon Wasp to start.

50
00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:55.680
[George] It was the very first recorded use of a rearview mirror on an automobile in human history, an innovation that would within a decade become standard safety equipment on every passenger vehicle manufactured across the globe.

51
00:07:55.680 --> 00:08:05.440
[George] Mechanics in the pits scoffed at the small glass mirror, betting that engine vibrations would shatter the lens within five laps of high-speed racing on the jarring bricks.

52
00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:17.680
[George] News of these extraordinary events traveled by telegraph and steam train across the nation, captivating readers in major eastern cities and inspiring future generations of pioneers and inventors.

53
00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:32.240
[George] Rival drivers initially protested Haron's solo entry, demanding that the American Automobile Association require every entrant to carry a riding mechanic to warn of approaching vehicles, until Haron unveiled his innovative mounted mirror.

54
00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:44.800
[George] At 10 o'clock on Memorial Day morning, Carl Fisher led the field in a stoddard Dayton touring car for the world's first rolling pace lap, pulling off the track as starter Fred Wagner unfurled the red flag.

55
00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:54.480
[George] Forty unmuffled racing engines roared simultaneously, shaking the grandstands with a concussive shockwave of noise and choking blue caster oil smoke.

56
00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:58.320
[George] The inaugural race was an epic trial of mechanical attrition.

57
00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:05.880
[George] The rough vitrified bricks acted like abrasive grinding wheels against the primitive, natural rubber pneumatic tires of the era.

58
00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:14.920
[George] Tire blowouts occurred every few laps, sending cars spinning into the wooden inner guardrills and scattering splinters of ash and oak across the track.

59
00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:27.560
[George] While rival drivers pushed their engines to their absolute mechanical limits, speeding at 80 miles an hour and blowing out tires every 20 laps, Ray Haron drove with calculated mathematical discipline.

60
00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:43.160
[George] He paced the Marmon Wasp at an even, controlled average speed of 75 miles per hour, conserving his engine bearings and babying his firestone tires so effectively that he made only four pit stops during the entire 500-mile marathon.

61
00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:53.800
[George] Haron maintained a rhythmic, steady cadence lap after lap, smoothly guiding the yellow racer through the treacherous oil slick turns while competitors pushed too hard and crashed.

62
00:09:53.800 --> 00:10:05.000
[George] The enduring lessons of this historic chapter continue to resonate across American culture, reminding us of the courage, sacrifice, and vision of those who shaped the destiny of our nation.

63
00:10:05.000 --> 00:10:18.520
[George] Pneumatic tires of the era were notoriously fragile, shedding treads under the intense friction of the hot paving bricks and forcing pit crews to perform exhausting tire changes using heavy iron spoons and manual air pumps.

64
00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:24.280
[George] As the race entered its final fifty miles, only a dozen battered cars remained running.

65
00:10:24.280 --> 00:10:33.960
[George] Haron took the lead, battling wheel-to-wheel against Ralph Mulford in a massive white lozier and David Bruce Brown in a monstrous 900 cubic inch fiat.

66
00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:46.400
[George] In the chaotic, manual scoring stand, where judges tracked cars using paper score sheets and spinning number wheels, a multicar crash on the front stretch caused temporary confusion among the scorers.

67
00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:50.640
[George] Mulford pitted for a tire change, and Haron surged ahead on the bricks.

68
00:10:50.880 --> 00:11:04.880
[George] At 4 23 in the afternoon, after six hours, 42 minutes, and eight seconds of grueling competition, Ray Harron guided the yellow Marmon Wasp across the chalk line, greeted by the waving green checkered flag.

69
00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:10.320
[George] His average speed for the 500 miles was 74.6 miles per hour.

70
00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:28.560
[George] Mulford and the Lozier team initially contested the finish, claiming they had made up a lap during the scoring confusion, but after an exhaustive overnight audit of the official timing tape, speedway judges confirmed Ray Haron as the rightful and undisputed winner of the inaugural Indianapolis 500.

71
00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:39.280
[George] The scoring wire hummed with telegraph clicks as officials audited the split times, confirming that Harron's meticulous strategy had earned him the premier crown of global motorsport.

72
00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:53.520
[George] The roaring crowd in the wooden grandstands cheered deliriously as the yellow Marmon Wasp crossed the finish line after six hours and 42 minutes of relentless racing, having averaged 74.6 miles per hour over the distance.

73
00:11:53.520 --> 00:12:08.480
[George] True to his word, Ray Haron collected his $10,000 winner's check, stepped out of the Marmon Wasp, and never drove in another competitive automobile race, retiring permanently to pursue a brilliant career as an automotive inventor.

74
00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:24.560
[George] Holding over 20 industrial patents for carburetors and braking systems, the Marmon Wasp, preserved in its original racing condition with its iconic dashboard mirror and yellow tail fin, remains the crown jewel of the Indianapolis Motor Speedway Museum.

75
00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:50.520
[George] The inaugural 1911 race established the Indianapolis 500 as the greatest spectacle in racing, a crucible of technological innovation that gave the world not only the rearview mirror, but balloon tires, aerodynamic streamlining, seat belts, and modern automotive safety engineering, proving that the courage of pioneer drivers on 3 million paving bricks helped build the modern world on wheels.

76
00:12:50.520 --> 00:13:01.800
[George] Modern race cars traveling at 230 miles an hour still race across the historic yard of bricks at the start-finish line, honoring the pioneer drivers of 1911.

77
00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:17.960
[George] The inaugural 500 established the Indianapolis Motor Speedway as the undisputed capital of world auto racing, accelerating the development of four-wheel brakes, streamlined aerodynamics, and advanced engine alloys throughout the 20th century.

78
00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:28.120
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

79
00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:36.040
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey east into Ohio to uncover the forgotten swamp that shaped a state, the Great Black Swamp.

